  "output": "I've now reviewed the full codebase. It's honest to say upfront: this is a very early-stage skeleton (only `/sources` and `/get-started` are wired to real data; `/runs`, `/conflicts`, `/rules`, `/audit` referenced in nav don't exist as pages yet, and no TanStack Query, polling, or client-side data fetching exists yet despite the comment mentioning it). Most of the five categories you asked about genuinely don't apply yet — I'd rather tell you that than invent problems. Here's what's real, plus guidance for what to watch as the app grows.\n\n## Real findings today\n\n**1. `countSourcesBySide` does more work than it needs (redundant computation)** — `lib/server/sources.ts:23-30`\n\n```ts\nexport async function countSourcesBySide() {\n  const all = await listSources(); // selects every column, zod-parses jsonb `config` on every row\n  return {\n    crm: all.filter((s) => s.kind === \"salesforce\" || s.kind === \"hubspot\").length,\n    ...\n  };\n}\n```\n\nIt only needs `kind`, but it pulls full rows (including `config` jsonb) and runs each one through `sourceSchema.parse`. Fine at today's row counts, wasteful once sources grow. Fix — query just the column and skip the shared function:\n\n```ts\nexport async function countSourcesBySide() {\n  await connection();\n  const rows = await getDb().select({ kind: sources.kind }).from(sources);\n  return {\n    crm: rows.filter((s) => s.kind === \"salesforce\" || s.kind === \"hubspot\").length,\n    warehouse: rows.filter((s) => s.kind === \"databricks\" || s.kind === \"snowflake\").length,\n    synthetic: rows.filter((s) => s.kind === \"synthetic\").length,\n  };\n}\n```\n\n**2. No request-level memoization on `listSources` (caching gap, latent duplicate-query risk)**\n\n`listSources()` hits Postgres on every call with no memoization. Right now no single request calls it twice, so there's no active bug — but `countSourcesBySide` and `listSources` are separate call sites hitting the same table, and the moment a layout or parallel segment needs both in one render, you get two round trips for the same data. Wrap it in React's `cache()` so duplicate calls within a request dedupe for free:\n\n```ts\nimport { cache } from \"react\";\n\nexport const listSources = cache(async (): Promise<Source[]> => {\n  await connection();\n  const rows = await getDb().select().from(sources).orderBy(sources.createdAt);\n  return rows.map((row) => sourceSchema.parse(row));\n});\n```\n\n## Categories with nothing to report yet (and why)\n\n- **N+1 queries** — only one query function exists (`listSources`, a single flat `select`). There's no per-row loop issuing follow-up queries. This becomes a real risk once `runs`/`entities`/`matches`/`conflicts` list views are built (e.g. fetching a run then looping to fetch each match's entities) — when you build those, batch with `inArray()` joins rather than per-row queries.\n- **Unnecessary React re-renders** — the app is almost entirely Server Components. The only client components (`NavRail`, `SignOutControl`, motion wrappers, `LandingNav`) are small, don't hold derived state, and don't re-render off unrelated parent updates. Nothing to fix.\n- **Memory leaks** — no `useEffect`, subscriptions, timers, or event listeners anywhere in the codebase to leak. The DB connection in `lib/db/index.ts` is correctly cached on `globalThis` to survive HMR without opening new pool connections — that's the one place a leak pattern could show up, and it's already handled correctly.\n- **Other caching opportunities** — no repeated expensive computation exists yet outside item 1.\n\nThe comment in `lib/server/sources.ts` says \"TanStack Query is reserved for run-status polling only\" — when you build that polling for `/runs`, that's where re-render and cache-staleness bugs actually tend to appear (e.g. polling interval not clearing on unmount, or query key not scoped per run). Worth a follow-up pass once that code lands.\n",
